  "rules": [
    "Model forecasts exhibit a severe upward bias during high-volatility regimes; when recent history shows a sharp spike (e.g., >80 EUR) followed by volatility, the model systematically underestimates the peak and overestimates the subsequent mean-reversion path.",
    "The model fails to capture immediate momentum reversals after spikes; true outcomes often continue rising for 3-5 days post-spike before correcting, whereas the model predicts a steady decline or plateau immediately.",
    "Sentiment history (0.00-0.25) is insufficient to trigger upward corrections in the model; even with slight positive sentiment signals during volatile periods, the forecast remains anchored to historical averages rather than reacting to price spikes.",
    "The 30-day horizon error is characterized by an 'overshoot' of volatility: the model predicts a smooth linear trend that misses the jagged nature of true outcomes, resulting in significant cumulative deviation when actual prices oscillate within a wider range than forecasted.",
    "When recent history shows a consolidation phase (72-76 EUR) followed by a spike, the model's forecast slope is too shallow to match the true outcome's acceleration rate."
  ]
}
